+  "title": "GOV-0055: BADF-WP-GIT-E — composition identity: a committed `git-composition` record (content-tree bound) that `badf_compose.py` recomputes and compares; composition-verification subskill",
+  "problem": "## Observed\n\n`badf-git`'s core invariant is `SOURCE_HEAD_GREEN != INTEGRATION_SAFE`, and its composition contract (`references/composition-and-staleness.md`) says every integration claim binds `target_ref`, `target_base_sha`, `source_ref`, `source_head_sha`, `merge_base_sha`, `merge_method`, `expected_result_tree`, `policy_epoch`, `test_set_epoch`; `docs/05` mandates the same for composed changes. Measured on `main` after GIT-D: CI's `badf_compose.py` **computes** all of it on every PR — `BADF COMPOSE: base X + candidate Y -> composed Z (tree T)` — and **records none of it**: the tuple lives in a job log. No PR carries a composition claim; nothing compares a claim with what CI recomputes; post-merge nothing compares the landed tree with the expected one. In this program the cost was concrete: three rebases across GIT-B/C/D each silently invalidated composition, detected only by manual re-running (GIT-D now detects *that the target moved*, but nothing binds *what the composed result was expected to be*).\n\nThe obvious record — \"commit the tuple inside the PR\" — is circular: the commit that adds the record changes the PR head and the result tree. BADF already solved that circularity once: the self-dossier binds **content**, diffing `base..HEAD` with `work/<WP>/` and `badf/lockfile.json` excluded, and compose verifies it on the composed tree. GIT-E applies the same construction to composition. GTGM v0.1 §10 (composition is the core invariant), §15 (`composition-verification` subskill), §17 **GIT-E — Composition**.\n\n## Expected\n\n**A composition claim the author commits and CI recomputes.**\n\n1. **`badf_compose.py --record <path>`** (host shape) writes, after composing, a `git-composition` record: `repository`, `target_ref` (`refs/heads/<default>`), `target_base_sha`, `source_ref` (the branch), `merge_base_sha`, `merge_method: squash`, `expected_result_tree` (the composed tree — informational, changes when the record is added), **`expected_content_tree`** (the composed tree with `work/<WP>/` and `badf/lockfile.json` removed — computed index-only in the scratch via `git rm -r --cached` + `git write-tree`; **this is the binding**), `policy_epoch`, `test_set_epoch: null` + non-coverage, `suite_pattern`, `observed_at`. Read-only for the source repository (the scratch is disposable); the record file is the only write and only where asked.\n2. **Compose verifies the claim on the composed tree**, in CI and locally: if `work/<WP>/evidence/G07/composition.json` exists for the work package the message names, then (a) the recorded `target_base_sha` must equal the base being composed onto — else **`BADF COMPOSE FAIL: composition record is stale: recorded for base X, composing onto Y — recompute with --record`** (the rebase failure mode, made a refusal); (b) the recomputed `expected_content_tree` must equal the recorded one — else **`composition record does not match the composed content`** (a content change after the claim); (c) `merge_method` must be `squash` and `target_ref` the default branch. No record → compose behaves exactly as today and prints `composition: no record (pre-GIT-E work package)` — backward compatible; requiring the record is a later decision (GIT-F/J).\n3. **The self-dossier indexes the record** as G07 evidence of a new type `composition` (artifact = the record; digest bound) when it exists; `dossier` accepts it structurally (unknown types are not refused today; HUMAN_REQUIRED dossiers skip per-type rules, which is exactly why the enforcement point is compose).\n4. **`composition-verification` subskill** (GTGM §15): when to compose, what to bind, the author's order of operations — *commit content → `badf_compose.py --record work/<WP>/evidence/G07/composition.json` → `self-dossier` → commit → push* — and the rule: a rebase or any content change **recomputes the record, never edits it**. Registered `IMPLEMENTED`, C1, `allowed_tools: []`; the `badf-git` root stays `DESIGNED`.\n5. **This PR is its own first subject:** it carries a composition record that CI's compose verifies — the positive control on real infrastructure.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `--record` writes a `git-composition` record with every field above; `expected_content_tree` is unaffected by the presence of the record, the lockfile, or anything under `work/<WP>/` (the circularity proof).\n2. Compose with a CURRENT record → PASS, printing `composition: CURRENT (content tree <T7>)`.\n3. Target moved after the record → compose **FAIL** naming both bases and `--record`.\n4. Tracked content changed after the record (outside the exclusions) → compose **FAIL** naming the content-tree mismatch.\n5. Only the record + lockfile + `work/<WP>/` files added after the record → still CURRENT.\n6. `merge_method != squash` or a foreign `target_ref` in the record → compose FAIL; a malformed record (not `record: git-composition`, missing fields) → compose FAIL naming the defect.\n7. No record → compose PASS as today with the `no record` line (backward compatibility guarded by a test on a WP without one).\n8. Two `--record` runs on the same base/candidate are identical modulo `observed_at`; the source repository is untouched (status/reflog/stash/index snapshots).\n9. The self-dossier indexes `composition` evidence when the record exists (and not otherwise); the dossier validates (`HELD`) with it; `dossier` never refuses the extra type.\n10. Each refusal branch **load-bearing** (mutation: base check dropped, content check dropped, merge-method check dropped, malformed accepted, exclusions dropped so the record poisons its own tree).\n11. `composition-verification` registered `IMPLEMENTED` C1 `allowed_tools: []` with a real digest; root stays `DESIGNED`; no new script, no schema change (the record is an artifact, the evidence object uses the existing `composition` slot only if needed), no authority/tool-registry change; `repo` clean; full `unittest discover` + composed-tree compose green in CI.\n12. **This PR's own